# Gravity-by-intention argument: the main conclusion is S1. S2-S3 and S5-S6 give the difficulties.
# A1 and A2 make explicit the link the text relies on between those difficulties and the lack of clear principles.
atom	easy_principles	it is easy to state clear principles for determining the gravity of crimes by the intention only
atom	intends_right_sometimes	sometimes, as with the patriotic rebel, the intention of the criminal is to do what is right and good
atom	no_harm_intended_many	in many cases the criminal does not intend to cause any actual harm to any sentient being
atom	secrecy_lessens_gravity	a crime is rendered less grave by being kept perfectly secret
atom	secondary_evil_great_part	a great part of the harm done by a crime is the secondary evil of the alarm and insecurity which it causes
atom	secrecy_cuts_off_secondary	the secondary evil is cut off by complete secrecy
atom	difficulty_practical	the difficulty about secrecy is a practical one
atom	punish_after_discovery	we are not called upon to punish a crime until it has been discovered, and then the secondary evil has been caused
atom	designed_for_discovery	the crime was designed for discovery
atom	secondary_evil_intended	the secondary evil caused by the crime was intended by the criminal
atom	gravity_tracks_intended_harm	the gravity of a crime can be measured by the harm the criminal intended
atom	utilitarian_reading	we say heinousness depends on the loss of happiness such acts would generally cause if unpunished, and suppose the criminal aware of this
atom	legal_fiction	we are forcing a utilitarian theory into an intuitional form by means of a legal fiction
